<Layout
  title="About — Shintaro Jokagi"
  description="Japanese-Kiwi software engineer and open-source maintainer based in Auckland. Self-taught builder, Yamada UI maintainer, Zen Browser website architect."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="eyebrow">About</div>
      <h1>
        A builder shaped by<br /><span style="color: var(--cobalt)"
          >movement</span
        >.
      </h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Born in Tokyo, raised across Japan, the Philippines, and New Zealand.
        Self-taught in code, curious about people, and happiest when something
        I've built helps someone else move faster.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-pad" style="padding-top: 1rem">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="page-card prose">
        <h2>The path</h2>
        <p>
          I was born in 2005 in Tokyo. After the 2011 earthquake, my family
          moved to Ehime. In 2017 I spent a year at an Auckland intermediate
          school, then three months in Cebu studying English, then a year at a
          British school in the Philippines. In 2019 I came back to New Zealand
          for good — Westlake Boys High School, then the University of Auckland.
        </p>
        <p>
          Along the way I tore my ACL three times playing football. The
          rehabilitation taught me patience, consistency, and how much of
          engineering — and life — is just showing up every day.
        </p>

        <h2>How I got into software</h2>
        <p>
          I started programming in 2020 with typing games and small web apps.
          Nobody taught me — I just kept shipping. By 2024 I was helping run an
          open-source design system, rebuilding a browser's website, and
          teaching Japanese kids to code in Minecraft over Zoom.
        </p>
        <p>
          That same year I joined the University of Auckland for a double major
          in Computer Science and IT Management, and started contributing to
          Yamada UI and Zen Browser in earnest.
        </p>

        <h2>What I do now</h2>
        <p>
          I'm a software engineer at <strong>Hazumi Inc.</strong>, building
          websites and product experiences remotely from Auckland for the Tokyo
          team. In open source I'm an official <strong>Yamada UI</strong>{' '}
          maintainer and the credited <strong>Core Website Architect</strong>{
            ' '
          }
          for the Zen Browser website.
        </p>
        <p>
          I care a lot about the last 10% of a product — the empty states, the
          focus rings, the sentence that makes someone smile, the PR description
          that helps the next reviewer. Great software is mostly about people.
        </p>

        <h2>Outside of work</h2>
        <p>
          Kendama, juggling, table tennis, badminton, football, and the
          occasional very overengineered side project. I also read a lot, write
          when I can, and keep comparing browsers like other people compare
          coffee shops.
        </p>

